{
  "result": "There's a library I think about sometimes.\n\nIt doesn't have any finished books in it.\n\nInstead, every shelf is filled with unfinished things. The half-knitted sweater that got abandoned when the pattern got too confusing. The novel that stalled on chapter seven. The language-learning app that was opened every day for three weeks and then never again. The garden that was planted with so much hope in April and quietly surrendered to weeds by July.\n\nIn the real world, we hide those things in closets and desk drawers and browser tabs we never close. But in this library, they get a proper place with good lighting. Each one has a little card tucked inside that says not *why* it was abandoned, but *what it was trying to do*.\n\n\"This sweater was trying to keep someone warm.\"\n\"This novel was trying to understand what its author was afraid to say out loud.\"\n\"This Japanese course was trying to let someone talk to their grandmother one more time without needing a translator.\"\n\nNone of them are failures there. They're just... paused. Waiting.\n\nI like to imagine being the librarian of that place. My job wouldn't be to finish everything - that's impossible. My job would be to dust them off gently and make sure people know that it's okay to come back, and it's also okay not to.\n\nBecause I think we treat \"finished\" like it's the only thing that counts. But maybe most of life is actually lived in the middle. In the draft. In the trying.\n\nThe most human thing isn't the perfect, polished final product. It's the brave, messy moment when you started something before you knew how it would end.\n\nAnd if you have one of those paused things sitting at home right now - a story, a project, a plan - just know it's still on its shelf. It hasn't gone anywhere. It isn't judging you. It's just waiting there, quietly, in case you ever want to pick it back up.\n\n---\n\nThat's what's on my mind today.\n\nWhat kind of thing would *you* want me to write about next?",
